 singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=12,$ ${\\sigma }_{2}=9,$ and ${\\sigma }_{3}=0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269243 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0157c09c-4c34-5a09-a678-edce758d03a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269243 #edb/ref 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=12,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={12}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}={12}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=12.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=12.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369741 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186369742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369741 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139544" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369741 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369741 #edb/ref 1071 "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=8,$ ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{5},$ and ${\\sigma }_{3}=0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369741 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dd29d329-0104-5ef8-b69c-cd8c21549714" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369741 #edb/ref 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=8,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={8}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}={8}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=8.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369742 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369742 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369742 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369742 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369742 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369742 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369742 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369742 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186369745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369742 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0b618b69-53f8-5721-89d5-02c9360f4137" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369743 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\sqrt{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369743 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186369743 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "07e2bd31